Tokens in shared directories like /shared can accidentally be leaked during deployment error analysis if scripts or logs print token file contents directly to output. For example:

# Deployment script snippet (vulnerable)
echo "Reading token from shared directory for auth check:"
cat /shared/token.txt  # Token printed in logs

# If authentication fails:
echo "Authentication failed with token:"
cat /shared/token.txt  # Token again logged

# Logs from this script may get copied to issue threads, exposing the secret

To avoid this, never print tokens directly. Instead, verify presence without printing value:

# Safer version
if [ -f /shared/token.txt ]; then
  echo "Token file exists in /shared, authentication proceeding"
else
  echo "Token file missing, authentication cannot proceed"
fi

# On failure, log generic error only
echo "Authentication failed. Please check token configuration securely."

This practice helps maintain secret confidentiality even during troubleshooting and prevents accidental leaks via issue threads or logs.

